  ],
  "text": "Ask what a cup of tea cost when your parents were your age. Prices rising feels like somebody's fault. Mostly it is the design. More money chasing the same goods, and each unit buys a little less than it did. Costs rise, demand rises, and both push in the same direction. So why not aim for zero instead? Because the other side of zero is much worse. If prices fall, everyone waits to buy. Waiting means less selling, which means fewer jobs. So central banks aim for a low steady rise instead. Uncomfortable, but stable. Which means cash sitting still loses value every year, on purpose, by design. Saving in cash is not neutral. It is a slow decision to hold something that shrinks. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
